      <h2 class="text-center section-title">Signs Your Court Needs Resurfacing</h2>
      <div class="content-grid">
        <div class="content-card">
          <h3>Surface Cracking</h3>
          <p>Cracks are the most obvious sign that resurfacing is needed. Small hairline cracks can be repaired, but extensive cracking indicates the need for complete surface renewal.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Hairline surface cracks</li>
            <li>Structural foundation cracks</li>
            <li>Expansion joint separation</li>
            <li>Settlement-related cracking</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <h3>Color Fading & Wear</h3>
          <p>UV exposure and weather conditions cause court colors to fade and surfaces to become worn, affecting both appearance and playing characteristics.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Significant color fading</li>
            <li>Uneven surface wear patterns</li>
            <li>Line marking deterioration</li>
            <li>Surface discoloration</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <h3>Rough or Slippery Texture</h3>
          <p>Surface texture changes can affect ball bounce, player traction, and safety. Both overly rough and excessively smooth surfaces indicate resurfacing needs.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Loss of surface texture</li>
            <li>Excessive surface roughness</li>
            <li>Inconsistent ball bounce</li>
            <li>Reduced player traction</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

